From c8d3d52a818b5111b5b19223f85edaa97395ff9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Jul 2026 08:10:34 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] api: idempotent, owner-tagged, node-serialized vxlan
 creation

Rework MakeVxLan from create-and-hope into ensure semantics:

- Every vxlan link is created with an ownership alias in IFLA_IFALIAS
  ('koko:vxlan:<owner-key>:<spec-key>', owner-key = hash(netns, ifname),
  spec-key = hash of the desired vxlan tuple). A legacy single-hash alias
  form is still recognized.
- If the target name already exists in the pod namespace: adopt it when it
  matches the desired vxlan tuple (bring it up, re-add addresses
  EEXIST-tolerantly); delete and recreate it when it is stale but carries
  our ownership alias; fail loudly when it is not ours.
- Host-side EEXIST during staging is resolved against the kernel's real
  uniqueness tuple (VNI, lower device, UDP port), not the transient staging
  name: an owned conflicting host link is deleted and creation retried
  (bounded attempts); an unowned conflict fails with a full conflict dump.
- All creators on the node (every CNI plugin invocation and meshnetd)
  serialize behind an in-process mutex plus a shared file lock
  (KOKO_VXLAN_LOCK override; default /opt/cni/bin/.koko-vxlan-create.lock,
  a hostPath shared by kubelet's plugin execution and the daemonset).
- SetVethLink failure after staging also removes the link by name in the
  target namespace instead of relying on a stale pre-move handle.

The old 'retry once and swallow file exists' behavior is gone; callers that
special-cased 'file exists' strings can rely on ensure semantics instead.

Core is upstreamable; the default lock path is deployment-specific and
overridable via KOKO_VXLAN_LOCK.
